From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 20:27:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/28] Saving and loading while playing: audio serviced in
 windows of 64, not every sample

Since v11, XMLSerializer::write() called AudioEngine::routineWithClusterLoading() (and the UI timers, OLED, PIC) every
256 characters, which the XML generation reaches every sample or two. That stopped the buzz of 1.2.1, but routine()
then rendered windows of about 24 samples, each paying its fixed cost (about what 20 samples of a busy song cost), so
the audio took nearly all the CPU and a save took many times longer than writing the XML. XMLDeserializer::xmlReadDone()
(every 64 reads, a few samples apart) had the same pattern for loading.

Both now call routineWhileAccessingFile(): it services only once 8 samples are due (AudioEngine::
getNumSamplesSinceOutputFilled(), one read of the DMA's position), and then routine() renders at least 64 samples
(AudioEngine::minNumSamplesToRender, applied after the usual doubling), ahead of what is due; the calls after that only
output them. The output buffer is refilled at the latest after the samples due plus the time one window of 64 takes.
Normal playback is unchanged (minNumSamplesToRender is 0 outside file access).

Emulator (tests/song, SAVE=1 run.sh: the firmware saves the playing song with the DMA in real time), v13 base -> this:
- 3 of 8 synths (about 50 % CPU), save at bar 2 (chord change): 344 ms -> 22 ms, worst gap 80 -> 71 samples
- 5 synths (about 70 % CPU), bar 2: 716 ms -> 66 ms, worst gap 128 (1 sample underrun) -> 97, no underrun
- mid-bar: 3 synths 80 -> 10 ms (gap 50 -> 56), 5 synths 180 -> 16 ms (gap 53 -> 66)
The XML written is identical; normal playback renders bit-exactly the same (demand run, --init-sounds --seed 1).

